 whether he was later  put on the track and then I heard anyway  I was so scared and I was so upset I  never saw anything like this in my life  I simply ran away and you know this I  see it personally is the greatest energy  of my life that you know there Jewish  people were deported or on me I didn't  do anything  I panicked I'm not even medic not even  fear I just didn't know what to do now  my second direct experience was  the crying in the night it was  summertime and it was one of those very  very quiet nights and I could not sleep  for some reason and there was enough  moisture in the air we were quite far  from the railroad station but somehow  there must have been enough moisture to  carry sand and I woke up to the sound of  many people my impression was literally  thousands of people crying I didn't know  what it was first it was it was a  terrifying impression and then I  realized this is the sound of people  crying I remember even reflecting you  know that must be children women men  everybody because it was that kind of a  chorus wailing and crying and next  morning I talked to the several other  people were still left in the in the  Jesuit house and asked them if they  heard something our housekeeper  custodian and they all said that they  heard it and I think it was the  custodian a gardener janitor who told me  that those were the Jewish people crying  because at our station the Hungarians  and armory handed them over to the SS  troops to be deported to Germany and  sometimes when this happened  the people would start to cry and that  would spread to a full train and this is  what we heard that night I didn't know  about the death camps as such at that  time and I didn't know about the ovens  the burning  but I personally at that moment I felt  persuasion coming up on me that these  people will be all killed and again you  see this is where I stress what I am  trying to say I wish I could live my  life today maybe I would be ready to  them run in front of the train and lay  down and I don't want to sound dramatic  maybe I would have today I would call  out or protest or risk being shot down  or club down and at that time I was  immobilized it was just a feeling not or  even a feeling of what can I do there is  nothing to do just running away simply  nothing away it was it was beyond my  experience I was utterly badly bad I  remember waking up on a Saturday morning  I heard shots again I quickly ran to my  window that was my my observation point  like my my eyes must have been three  times as big this as they are now  because I remember spending lots of  hours in that window  I ran to it and I looked around and I  saw dead people all around and on the  square about an hour later I heard  German voices  raus raus raus that was one word that I  learned very quickly I meant out out our  to us was like a door like dogs barking  and  they were German voices telling  everybody all Jews out all Jews out of  the square we heard people beginning to  cry and chaos where everybody was  running up and down in my building my  cousin finally started yelling to my  family and whoever because people  started running into the hiding place  and out nobody knew what fight what to  do some of us who got into the hiding  place  my cousin finally shut the door and said  all right now you're in and nobody out I  myself started crying and I said I  wanted to go with everybody else my dad  also wanted to go but he closed the door  he closed the little door underneath and  he said nobody's moving out of here as a  matter of fact he I remember him saying  distinctly he said if you're going to  die you gotta die right here you're not  going to walk to you death I started  crying and but then he made me shut up  and then I looked out through there was  the only opening for for air was there  was like a porthole here under the roof  and I looked out and I saw lots of Jews  with little packages walking towards the  square and I also saw my grandfather my  other grandfather Matt from my father's  side who lived in a different building  and his room and my grandma coming over  and they were looking up and deciding  whether to come to come up and hide with  us or to go away and finally I saw the  mark again walk away and I never saw  them again  I heard I heard people yelling and  screaming and crying and shooting and a  hoedown so but when we went to my donek  this was just the most terrible as much  as I experienced bad things after but  this was they put us in cattle cars and  they pushed in I don't know how many  people but they really wanted us to die  and we warned us those wagons um I don't  know how many people were there but my  daughter died in my arms  my younger brother was  and my husband's two sisters it's not  enough oxygen folders people and they  kept us in the swag ins for days they  wanted us to die in the wagons you know  the kettle class with very little  windows how old was your brother maybe  13  he wasn't about my family so you know  when my mother died my nerves and said  to myself I ain't gonna live I must be  the only one survivor for my family I'm  gonna live  I made up my mind I'm gonna defy you I'm  not gonna give it because he wants me to  die I'm gonna live I'm going to just be  very very strong and this is something  really nightmarish  when we came to that camp I can probably  describe something for somebody who  hasn't seen it  but has seen the film the apocalypse now  we came at night and were surrounded by  a group of people with yellow faces  begging for scraps of bread covered with  blankets  and typical muscle man's  as we found out the following day work  see  was fabricating the chemicals name EP  clean to fill the shelves  this is the explosive thing that  explodes in their artillery shells and  working with that chemical your lifespan  is only three four months your skin  turns yellow you shrivel and this will  be patron airs surrounding us  ouch wits if I would like to describe it  I would say that is there has not been  that has not been people did not invent  an expression what ash which was it was  hell on earth and  the silence of Auschwitz was held  the nights were hell  and the day's somehow we got up at 3 o  clock in the morning and at 4 o'clock  summer time or 4:30 when the Sun came up  it was not like the Sun I swear to you  it was not bright  it was always read to me it was always  black to me it never said never never  was life to me it was destruction the  Sun was never beautiful and when the  moon was out it meant only destruction  we almost forgot what life was all about
 

